WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.000
What does Elektra bring to the&nbsp;
table that Captain America can't?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.240
Is Victor von Doom really a better&nbsp;
villain than Ultron? We love the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:12.960
MCU – we're not knocking it one bit – but the&nbsp;
old Marvel movies just did some things better.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:17.600
There isn't a superhero film out there,&nbsp;
at least on the Marvel side of things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:22.160
that tells a more compelling and accurate&nbsp;
origin story than the original "Spider-Man."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:25.840
Not only does it carry the heart of the&nbsp;
original Marvel comic books on its sleeve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:30.800
it combines the origin directly with&nbsp;
one of Spidey's most formidable foes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:36.080
Likewise, the 2005 "Fantastic Four" film, Ang&nbsp;
Lee's "Hulk," and "The Punisher," among others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:40.640
set their title characters up for success&nbsp;
early on. How is this done? By establishing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:46.160
a clear origin story that often directly ties&nbsp;
our heroes to their most iconic villains.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:50.960
While MCU films like "Iron Man" and "Captain&nbsp;
America: The First Avenger" stand out as solid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:55.600
origin stories, most MCU films skip&nbsp;
their inception completely. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:59.440
the films usually dive right into the action&nbsp;
to keep their audience's attention. That,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:03.440
or characters are introduced in another&nbsp;
film before getting their own standalone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:09.280
which also often skip the origin stories. This&nbsp;
can be a huge disservice to both the characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:14.480
and the audience. Peter Parker watching his Uncle&nbsp;
Ben die, Bruce Banner's childhood flashbacks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:19.840
and Logan's frustrated attempts to uncover his own&nbsp;
history are what grounds and guides the heroes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:25.040
Plus, who doesn't love watching the Fantastic&nbsp;
Four as they get hit with cosmic rays from space?

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:26.800
"Rise and shine, big boy. How you feeling?"

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:31.040
There's no doubt that one of the things&nbsp;
we love most about movies like "Guardians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:34.960
of the Galaxy" and "Thor: Ragnarok" is the&nbsp;
humor. The blend of character growth with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:40.400
self-deprecating jokes is a true-blue staple&nbsp;
of the MCU, and for good reason — it works!

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.680
"I used to spin it really fast&nbsp;
and it would pull me off the…"

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:45.760
"Oh my God, the hammer pulled you off?"

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:46.880
"The ground."

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:52.000
In fact, many of us rewatch these films over again&nbsp;
just for the laughs. But the old Marvel films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.880
took a different approach. While there are clear&nbsp;
moments of levity in "X-Men," "Spider-Man," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:01.920
"Daredevil," the films don't allow the humor to&nbsp;
hold them back from telling more refined stories.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:05.280
Raimi's "Spider-Man" trilogy is the&nbsp;
perfect example of blending Spidey's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:09.280
humor with compelling stories that&nbsp;
feel honest, raw, and genuinely human.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:14.800
"I will… always be spiderman."

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:19.680
The "X-Men" series developed Hugh Jackman's&nbsp;
Wolverine in ways that no MCU character has,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:24.400
simply by taking the character and his mythos&nbsp;
seriously rather than making a joke out of them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:27.920
The only reason an incredible film&nbsp;
like "Logan" could have even been made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:31.200
is because of the groundwork they'd&nbsp;
laid with the character years prior.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:36.640
There's a lot to be said about a complex,&nbsp;
interconnected universe where Howard the Duck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:41.520
can chill with an immortal Elder and 20 years'&nbsp;
worth of Peter Parkers can meet up to make meta&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:46.080
jokes about their various movies' faults. It's&nbsp;
a whole lot of fun for fans, but it also means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:50.240
you have to keep up with dozens of releases&nbsp;
to understand everything that's going on.

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:55.200
One thing the old Marvel movies really had going&nbsp;
for them was that they were truly stand-alone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:59.040
You didn't have to watch "Ghost Rider"&nbsp;
to understand a scene or two in "X-Men:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:03.920
The Last Stand," and that's something of a&nbsp;
lost art nowadays. We've all gotten so used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:08.720
to interconnected stories and universes&nbsp;
that we expect them now at every turn.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:13.520
Aside from the "X-Men" and "Blade" films, which&nbsp;
really build off each previous installment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:18.160
most of the old Marvel flicks can be consumed&nbsp;
on their own. "Spider-Man 2" is just as great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:23.040
if you haven't seen the first film as it is if you&nbsp;
have, and even "Elektra" isn't completely bound by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:27.840
the "Daredevil" film she first appeared in. If&nbsp;
"Captain America: Civil War" proved anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:32.000
it's that sometimes being too interconnected&nbsp;
can hurt the growth of your primary characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.600
and that's not something the old Marvel&nbsp;
films had to worry about. Rather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:40.160
each made sure to focus on their own localized&nbsp;
story before expanding the world around them.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:45.520
Since the foundation of the MCU, the franchise&nbsp;
has struggled primarily with their villains.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:51.040
Aside from Loki, Thanos, Killmonger, Vulture,&nbsp;
and one or two baddies from the Netflix shows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:55.840
many MCU villains aren't terribly compelling,&nbsp;
interesting, or even believable. Most of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:01.040
don't stand out as anything other than pale&nbsp;
imitations of their heroes, and not in a good way.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:05.840
The old Marvel films, however, understood their&nbsp;
villains completely. Our heroes' main foes were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:10.080
dark, mirky reflections of themselves, and&nbsp;
not just because they had a similar Stark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:14.880
Tech suit of armor. Rather, they made similar&nbsp;
choices to our heroes but with different results,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:19.520
often warping their own perspectives of truth and&nbsp;
justice to fit their own agendas. The dichotomy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:24.560
between characters like Spider-Man and Green&nbsp;
Goblin, Professor X and Magneto, and Reed Richards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:29.920
and Victor von Doom is fascinating, powerful,&nbsp;
and full of surprises. In fact, some of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:34.720
villains are so iconic that they even brought a&nbsp;
bunch of them back for "Spider-Man: No Way Home."

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:36.560
"Attaboy."

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:42.160
The old Marvel movies also dealt with a completely&nbsp;
different set of philosophical implications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:47.520
than most in the MCU. While "Iron Man" focuses&nbsp;
heavily on "War On Terror" politics and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:52.000
"Captain America" films reference PATRIOT Act&nbsp;
surveillance, earlier Marvel pictures went for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:57.760
more universal, down-to-earth themes. They dove&nbsp;
into sociology, philosophy, and even religion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:02.800
Like the original Marvel Comics before them, the&nbsp;
"X-Men" trilogy ran underlying themes of equality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:06.080
hoping to remind us that people should&nbsp;
be treated the same whether they have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:10.400
difference in appearance or ability. This was&nbsp;
ultimately the message that Stan Lee hoped to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:14.240
convey with their famed mutant characters,&nbsp;
and the Fox films handled it pretty well.

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:17.600
The "Spider-Man" trilogy heavily&nbsp;
promotes moral notions of responsibility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:21.920
which stand out among all superhero films&nbsp;
as being some of the most powerful ideas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:26.560
explored in the genre. The "Daredevil" film&nbsp;
even touches on Matt Murdock's Catholicism and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:30.640
how his role as Hell's Kitchen's "Guardian&nbsp;
Devil" is heavily influenced by his faith.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:35.680
"Ghost Rider" explores the moral and spiritual&nbsp;
implications of selling one's soul. Likewise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:40.560
"Fantastic Four," much like the MCU's "Guardians&nbsp;
of the Galaxy" now, promotes the idea that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:45.600
can find your "true family" through friendships.&nbsp;
They weren't always the best films, but there's no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:50.400
denying that the old Marvel films had a depth&nbsp;
to them that has yet to be fully replicated.

